Per zod@4.5.4:

z.object({ username: z.string(), xp: z.number() }) builds the schema; `.parse()` returns a deeply cloned, strongly-typed result on success and throws a `ZodError` on failure. `.safeParse()` instead returns a discriminated union: `result.success` (boolean) with `result.data` on success or `result.error` (a `ZodError`) on failure. The `ZodError.issues` is an array of objects, each with at least `message` (string), `path` (array of property keys), plus `expected` and `code`. Note: the provided content shows `z.string()`/`z.number()` but does not document `z.email()` vs `z.string().email()` or `z.number().int().min().max()`.

Source excerpt:
> To avoid a `try/catch` block, you can use the `.safeParse()` method to get back a plain result object containing either the successfully parsed data or a `ZodError`. The result type is a [discriminated union](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/narrowing.html#discriminated-unions), so you can handle both cases conveniently.
> 
> ```ts
> const result = Player.safeParse({ username: 42, xp: "100" });
> if (!result.success) {
>   result.error; // ZodError instance
> } else {
>   result.data; // { username: string; xp: number }
> }
> ```

